Stop the file monitor listing a directory to resolve a literal filename.

cabal-install monitors every `packages:` entry so a later invocation can
tell whether the project needs re-elaborating:
`ProjectConfig.checkIsFileGlobPackage` -> `RebuildMonad.matchFileGlob`,
which registers `monitorFiles [monitorFileGlobExistence glob]`.  Turning
that into monitor state runs `FileMonitor.buildMonitorStateGlobRel`, which
listed the containing directory unconditionally -- before even looking at
which `Glob` constructor it had -- and then filtered the result.  The probe
side does the same: when a monitored directory's mtime changes, it re-lists
and re-filters.

The cost is therefore O(entries in the directory) per `packages:` entry,
however specific that entry is.  A generated cabal.project whose entries are
absolute paths into one large directory rescans that directory once per
entry.  Concretely, 31 entries of the form `/nix/store/<hash>-<name>-src`
list a /nix/store of ~362k names 31 times, decoding every name each time.
Page-cached on a local disk that is merely wasteful; over a virtiofs share
(a Linux builder VM on a macOS host) each 2KB getdents64 is a round trip to
the host and the command stops making observable progress at all -- no CPU,
no major faults, just permanent `request_wait_answer`.

Add `matchGlobEntries`: when the `GlobPieces` is a single `Literal`, stat
the one candidate instead of enumerating its parent, since at most one entry
can match.  Anything that can match more than one entry keeps the listing.

Both the build side and the probe side go through it, and that matters:
they have to agree on what counts as a match, or a literal recorded by one
and rejected by the other reads as a deleted file on every check.

Measured with a harness calling `updateFileMonitor` directly over 31
`packages:`-shaped globs into a 40,033-entry directory, warm cache, APFS
(the real case is ~9x larger again):

    unpatched   1.306s  1.371s  1.361s  1.349s
    patched     0.0042s 0.0044s 0.0041s 0.0044s

Two notes for review.

  * This is not something laziness can fix.  `getDirectoryContents` reads
    the whole directory inside a `bracket` before returning -- directory's
    `getDirectoryContentsInternal` is a strict accumulator loop, and the
    FilePath wrapper then `traverse`s the result to decode every name;
    there is no `unsafeInterleaveIO` anywhere in the package -- and both
    callers forced the entire spine anyway, via `sort` and `filterM`.

  * The listing branch compares the pattern against the name the
    filesystem reports, using exact string equality, so on a
    case-insensitive or name-normalising filesystem (APFS, HFS+, NTFS) a
    literal differing from the on-disk entry only in case does not match,
    where the stat accepts it.  That is already how
    `Distribution.Simple.Glob` resolves a literal -- `splitConstantPrefix`
    turns a trailing `GlobFile [Literal f]` into a single
    `doesDirectoryExist`/`doesFileExist` -- so this brings the monitor into
    line with the matcher that decided the glob matched in the first place,
    rather than away from it.

For the record, this is NOT the same change as patching
`Distribution.Simple.Glob`.  A literal fast path there is dead code: the
`literalize` step above already handles it, and the glob parser rejects `*`
in a directory component (`StarInDirectory`) and requires `**/` to be
followed by a wildcard filename (`LiteralFileNameGlobStar`), so nothing can
hand `runDirFileGlob`'s `go` a bare literal to be fast about.  Patched and
unpatched Cabal produced identical results and identical timings on every
glob shape that parses.

A plain upstream bug, so it should go to cabal rather than live here
indefinitely.

This is the stable-haskell fork's copy of the change; ../../nix-tools carries
the same fix against cabal-install 3.16.  The two diffs differ because the
fork's FileMonitor is the 3.17-era refactor: it shares its probe code through
`probeMonitorStateFiles` / `probeMonitorStateDirs`, and it has a
`GlobDirRecursive` branch, which keeps the listing -- a `**` glob has to
enumerate whatever happens, and `probeMonitorStateDirs` passes `Nothing` for
the filename glob in that case.  Keep the two in step.

